{
  "teamId": 139,
  "year": 2022,
  "narrative": "The 2022 Tampa Bay Rays were a team of quiet resilience and frustrating inconsistency, never quite finding the sustained dominance that had defined their best recent seasons yet scrapping their way to 86 wins and a playoff berth. April set an encouraging tone — Kevin Kiermaier's walk-off two-run blast on the 23rd was the kind of thunderclap moment that signals a team with fight, and the Rays finished the month a solid 12-9. May brought more of the same split personality: Manuel Margot was everywhere as a clutch performer, but the bullpen — particularly Andrew Kittredge — began showing cracks that would define Tampa Bay's ceiling all year.\n\nJune was the season's most turbulent month, a 12-14 stretch that exposed real vulnerabilities. Relievers gave up critical home runs almost weekly, and only occasional flashes like Taylor Walls' three-run shot or Isaac Paredes' clutch two-run single in the final days of the month kept the Rays from falling out of contention entirely. July was similarly grinding — the bullpen, especially Colin Poche, continued to hemorrhage big hits — yet the Rays stayed afloat at 14-13, a testament to a pitching staff anchored by one of the best ERAs in the American League.\n\nAugust was the season's defining chapter. The Rays went 18-9, their best month by a wide margin, turning close games into wins with timely doubles from Brandon Lowe, Francisco Mejía, and Harold Ramírez. Even a Josh Donaldson grand slam off Jalen Beeks couldn't derail the momentum for long. September cooled back to .500, but the Rays had done enough to earn a Wild Card spot, setting up a date with the Cleveland Guardians in October.\n\nThe postseason was brief and bittersweet. Shane McClanahan pitched well enough to win in Game 1, and Jose Siri's home run gave fans a moment to believe, but José Ramírez's blast off McClanahan changed the complexion of the series. Game 2 brought more heartbreak — Oscar Gonzalez crushed a decisive home run off Corey Kluber, and the Rays' bats went quiet when it mattered most. A team that had survived so many regular-season gut punches ran out of answers against a hungry Cleveland club, closing the book on a season that was good but always felt like it could have been something more.",
